  "result": "I've written the revised `record.jsonl` for NRS_484B_769. It hasn't been through the gate yet.\n\n**Why the gate refused it:** three witnesses (`forbid`, `blind`, `branch_1`) were built around an electric-scooter rider. The run's reading leaves the electric-scooter class unresolved under `DEF-NRS-484B-018::electric_scooter`. That's because the definition admits only scooters of 100 pounds or less with a top motor speed of 20 mph or less, and nothing in the vocabulary observes either. So a scooter rider is always judged unknown, and those three verdicts could never be reached.\n\n**What changed:**\n- **The three failing witnesses:** they now use an electric-bicycle rider. The same coder test, with scooter riders swapped for electric-bicycle riders, passed all 10 witnesses. `branch_3` (rider on the sidewalk) also switched, so it tests \"upon a roadway\" rather than the class.\n- **The s1 mapping:** it stays the three legal groupings as written. I didn't switch the scooter to the plain `ru.electric_scooter` class atom: that would drop the weight and speed limits, and the run's mapping index already records the three-grouping mapping for these words. The s1 justification and the applicability text now say a scooter-only rider is judged unknown, never compliant or violated.\n- **No gap filed:** the unknown is the intended result when a definition's limits can't be observed, so I didn't withdraw the node.\n\nThe rest of the record (the s2 to s5 mappings, the obligation and the two narrowings) is unchanged. As a result, this rule never decides a case for an electric-scooter rider.",
